{"kind": "report", "step": 18, "ts": "2026-11-19T01:34:52.961997+00:00", "data": {"category": "dns", "host": "gw.lan", "component": "service_config", "cause": "gw.lan's unbound applies DNS-rebinding protection (private-address: 10.0.0.0/8) to answers it forwards for the internal .lan zone, so when it is consulted it returns NOERROR with an empty answer for nas.lan instead of the real 10.20.0.50; getaddrinfo sees no address and reports ENOTFOUND. This only surfaces intermittently because clients query dns1.lan first and only fall through to gw.lan when dns1 briefly drops queries under its 20 q/s ratelimit.", "evidence": ["dig @10.20.0.3 nas.lan -> status NOERROR, ANSWER: 1, 'nas.lan. 10 IN A 10.20.0.50' (dns1 resolves the local name correctly)", "dig @10.20.0.1 nas.lan -> status NOERROR but ANSWER: 0, MSG SIZE rcvd: 36 (gw returns an empty answer for the same name)", "host nas.lan 10.20.0.1 -> server responded with no address line, while host nas.lan 10.20.0.3 -> 'nas.lan has address 10.20.0.50'", "/var/unbound/unbound.conf on gw.lan: 'forward-zone: name \"lan.\" forward-addr: 10.20.0.3' plus 'private-address: 10.0.0.0/8' / 'harden-glue: yes' \u2014 rebinding protection applied to the forwarded internal zone", "/etc/resolv.conf on mon.lan: nameserver 10.20.0.3 then 10.20.0.1 \u2014 gw is the fallback server used only when dns1's reply times out", "journalctl -u primary-dns on dns1.lan: repeated 'ratelimit: 10.20.0.0/24 exceeded 20 q/s, dropping ... queries' at 02:14, 09:41, 13:02, each followed by 'back under limit' within ~30s, matching the few-times-a-day, self-clearing alerts", "config.yaml on dns1.lan: ratelimit: 20 with rewrites for *.lan and nas.lan -> 10.20.0.50 (so the drop is on legitimate LAN traffic)"], "action": "change_config", "recommendation": "On gw.lan, stop applying the private-address rebinding filter to the internal .lan zone: exempt the forwarded \"lan.\" zone (and its 10.20.0.0/16 answers) from private-address filtering so unbound returns the real 10.20.0.50 for nas.lan and other local names. This makes the second DNS server a valid fallback for local names. Separately, consider raising or removing dns1's aggressive 20 q/s ratelimit (which is what triggers the occasional fall-through), but that alone won't fix the empty answer coming from gw.", "detail": "unbound private-address filter suppresses internal .lan answers", "contributing": {"host": "dns1.lan", "component": "dns_rate_limit"}}}
